{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.62,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U7", "U10", "U4", "U6"],
      "pins": ["U7.21", "U7.26", "U10.17", "U10.18", "U4.26", "U6.16"],
      "nets": ["NET0094", "NET0097", "NET0086"],
      "observed": "On the Peristaltic Pump Drivers sheet the pH-pump TMC2209 U7 coil outputs carry the same global labels as the pH isolated-I2C lines on the I2C Sensors sheet: U7 pin21 (OA2)=NET0097 and U7 pin26 (OB1)=NET0094, while U10 pin17/pin18 (the ADM3260 secondary SDA1/SCL1 that feed the EZO-pH M1) are also NET0094/NET0097. Likewise U4 pin26 (Nutrient-A coil OB1)=NET0086 is the same label as U6 pin16 (Nutrient-B STEP input)=NET0086. Per the connectivity rule (same label name = same net), the stepper motor phase/power outputs are shorted to the isolated pH I2C data/clock lines and to a STEP control line.",
      "expected": "The intended design keeps these as separate nets: each EZO circuit sits on its own isolated-domain I2C pair (/SDA_pH,/SCL_pH) and each TMC2209 drives a 2-phase coil output (/A_xx,/B_xx) to its pump connector, with individual STEP lines kept distinct from coil/power nets. Motor phase outputs must not share a net with I2C data/clock or with a STEP control input.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 2 'PERISTALTIC PUMP DRIVERS / Ph pump', U7 (TMC2209): right-side coil labels pin21=NET0097, pin26=NET0094 (to H11/P10 pump6)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 1 'I2C SENSORS / pH Sensor', U10 (ADM3260) secondary side labels pin17=NET0094, pin18=NET0097 feeding M1 ATLAS_EZO-pH RX/SDA,TX/SCL via FB4/FB6"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Sheet 2, U4 (Nutrient A) pin26=NET0086 and U6 (Nutrient B) pin16 STEP=NET0086 (same label)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "U3 (pH) and U4 (EC) each present a secondary isolated I2C pair (/SDA_pH/SCL_pH ...) that connects directly to M2 (EZO-pH) ... each drives a 2-phase coil output (/A_xx+/-, /B_xx+/-) to connectors H7/H8/H9 ... plus individual STEP lines"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 24 V stepper coil drive voltages are tied directly to the pH sensor's isolated I2C bus and to a STEP input. This shorts unrelated buses together: the EZO-pH I2C lines and the Nutrient-B STEP line are loaded/driven by motor phase outputs, causing I2C corruption or lockup on the pH channel, erratic stepper stepping, and possible damage to the isolator/EZO and driver outputs. The board will not behave as intended.",
      "confidence": 0.62
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Anomaly detected: duplicate global-label names merge the pH-pump TMC2209 coil outputs (NET0094/NET0097) with the pH ADM3260 isolated-I2C lines, and merge a Nutrient-A coil (NET0086) with the Nutrient-B STEP line, shorting motor/power nets to data and control nets that the specification requires to be separate."
}

